CREATED-BY: WriteLog V12.83A
SOAPBOX: We are pleased to exceed our M2 score from last year, which set the World M2 Record.
SOAPBOX: Equipment problems this year will probably mean we aren't first in M2 and no new
SOAPBOX: record for us.
SOAPBOX: We had a good start on 80m and 40m, considering it was 5 AM in the morning local
SOAPBOX: time, and only dedicated contesters and the late night Asian operators were on.
SOAPBOX: As daylight arrived we moved up to 20m/10m and 15m.
SOAPBOX: 10 hours in, the amplifier failed for 20m and 10m - long before we had completed
SOAPBOX: our fill. The amp had been "indicating" strange, but had been putting our RF
SOAPBOX: just fine up to then.
SOAPBOX: The 15m amp was fine, so we continued with a lop-sided KW and 100w M2 operation.
SOAPBOX: We got to 40m perhaps a bit late, and had a high rate of 1-point contacts. 80m
SOAPBOX: struggled with only 100w.
SOAPBOX: At 20 hours in, decode problems started, and one radio stopped transmitting.
SOAPBOX: Only one operator was running both stations, so he got distracted from rate
SOAPBOX: trying to solve the problems. We limped to the finish line with a new record
SOAPBOX: score for us, but probably 2nd place in M2.
SOAPBOX: The other "technical equipment" worked fine, the VPN, station-site PC,
SOAPBOX: logging, Antennas, switches, filters, rotators, ... Thank you K6TD for
SOAPBOX: the work this summer to keep things running.
SOAPBOX: Thank you for the contacts!
SOAPBOX: K6TD Kevin
SOAPBOX: K6UFO Mark
SOAPBOX: ND2T Tom
SOAPBOX: NN6U John
SOAPBOX: W0YK Ed
SOAPBOX: from the mighty K6MTU station on Mt. Umunhum, CA
SOAPBOX: two Flex 6700 with SmartSDR
SOAPBOX: two Alpha 9500 amplifiers
SOAPBOX: 80m 4-square array
SOAPBOX: JK 4 el 40m yagi at 110 feet
SOAPBOX: JK Mid-Tri yagis at 95 and 45 feet high
